== 7 Recovery of the prescribed Kuga–Satake tensor <final:section>

The comparison of the preceding section and the domination by curves give an algebraic injection from the auxiliary K3 cohomology into a tensor product of two curve cohomologies. This does not yet realize the standard tensor required by Theorem 1. We bridge the difference by proving that its compositions with rational Hodge maps on the two curve factors, together with their interchanges, span every Hodge map into the tensor square of the full even Clifford module. This permits us to select the prescribed right factor and polarization. The calculation applies at the construction periods selected in Lemma 29, where the Hodge group is the full special orthogonal group. At the end of the section we transfer the curve source by a rational Hodge isometry; Section 8 will use that transfer to obtain seeds with $"NS" = P$ and extend one fixed tensor to every ample $P$-polarized target.

=== 7.1 An algebraic embedding into a tensor of curve cohomologies <an-algebraic-embedding-into-a-tensor-of-curve-cohomologies>

#thm([Lemma 7.1.])[
  #emph[Choose the abelian data and the pencil as in Lemma 16, and then choose $b$ in the common good open outside the countable exceptional sets of that lemma and Corollary 28. Then there are smooth projective curves $C_1 \, C_2$ and a rational algebraic correspondence 
$ Z in "CH"^2 \( S_b^0 times C_1 times C_2 \)_(bb(Q)) $

 whose action restricts to an injection 
$ f : U_b arrow.r H^1 \( C_1 \, bb(Q) \) ⊗ H^1 \( C_2 \, bb(Q) \) . $

 Here $U_b = T_(S_b^0)$ and $"Hg" \( U_b \) = "SO" \( U_b \)$.]
] <final:curves>

#proof[
  Let $Theta_b in "CH"^2 \( S_b^0 times X_b \)_(bb(Q))$ be the correspondence of Corollary 28; its restriction to $U_b$ is the specified identification $phi_b : U_b arrow.r U_(X_b)$. At these periods $U_(X_b)$ has no nonzero rational $\( 1 \, 1 \)$-class by Proposition 11. Proposition 15 therefore supplies curves $C_1 \, C_2$, a morphism $g : C_1 times C_2 arrow.r X_b$, and the algebraic correspondence
  $ Z_X = #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] pi_1^(C_1) times.square pi_1^(C_2) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] compose zws^t Gamma_g compose e in "CH"^2 \( X_b times C_1 times C_2 \)_(bb(Q)) \, $
  where the curve projectors are those constructed there. Its action on $U_(X_b)$ is the injection $g^(*)$, with image entirely in $H^1 \( C_1 \, bb(Q) \) ⊗ H^1 \( C_2 \, bb(Q) \)$. Set
  $ Z = Z_X compose Theta_b . $
  Its action on $U_b$ is $f = g^(*) phi_b$ and is injective. The composition passes through the surface $X_b$, so its codimension is $2 + 2 - 2 = 2$. The equality $U_b = T_(S_b^0)$ and the full Hodge group follow from Propositions 11 and 17 at these selected periods. ◻
]

The comparison just constructed fits into

$ \( U_b = T_(S_b^0) \, q_Y \) arrow.r^(med phi_b = \( Theta_b \)_(*) med) \( U_(X_b) \, q_X \) arrow.r^(med g^(*) med) H^1 \( C_1 \, bb(Q) \) ⊗ H^1 \( C_2 \, bb(Q) \) . $

Here $q_X$ is the cup-product form on $X_b$, and $q_Y$ is the form inherited from $Y$ in Proposition 9; the latter is exactly the K3 cup-product form by Proposition 17. The resolved pullback satisfies $phi_b^(*) q_X = 2 q_Y$. Thus the first map is an algebraic Hodge identification with a factor-two change of form, and the second is the algebraic injection supplied by the curves. The rational isometry used below concerns $q_Y$, not the rescaled form from $X_b$.

=== 7.2 The Clifford map and its tensor form <the-clifford-map-and-its-tensor-form>

We recall the tensor of Section 1 for an eight-dimensional quadratic space $\( U \, q \)$. With the Clifford convention $v^2 = q \( v \, v \)$, the full even module is

$ W = C^(+) \( U \, q \) \, #h(2em) dim_(bb(Q)) W = 2^7 = 128 . $

If $e_1 \, e_2$ form an oriented orthonormal basis of the positive period plane, with $U^(2 \, 0) = bb(C) \( e_1 + i e_2 \)$, put $J = e_1 e_2$. Then $J^2 = - 1$, and the Kuga–Satake Hodge circle acts on $W_(bb(R))$ by left multiplication by $exp \( theta J \)$. Conjugation by the same element acts on $U^(2 \, 0)$ by $exp \( 2 i theta \)$, as required by the weight-two Hodge structure.

Fix the prescribed rational vector $w in U$ with $q \( w \, w \) eq.not 0$. The standard embedding on the even module is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.1)")
 $ j_w : U \( 1 \) arrow.r "End" \( W \) \, #h(2em) j_w \( v \) \( a \) = v a w quad \( a in C^(+) \( U \, q \) \) . $ <final:specified-clifford>]

The output is even, and $w^(- 1) = w \/ q \( w \, w \)$ shows, by evaluation at $a = 1$, that $j_w$ is injective. For $g in "Spin" \( U \, q \)$ and left multiplication $L_g$ one has

$ L_g j_w \( v \) L_g^(- 1) \( a \) = \( g v g^(- 1) \) a w = j_w \( g v g^(- 1) \) \( a \) . $

Thus $j_w$ is a rational Hodge map for every period; the right factor $w$ is fixed and need not have any particular Hodge type.

Let $E$ be the prescribed rational polarization of $W = H^1 \( A \, bb(Q) \)$. With $"End" \( W \) = W ⊗ W^or$, its identification $I_E = \( E^flat \( 1 \) \)^(- 1) : W^or arrow.r^tilde.op W \( 1 \)$ converts $j_w$ into

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.2)")
 $ iota_(w \, E) : U arrow.r W ⊗ W subset H^2 \( A times A \, bb(Q) \) . $ <final:specified-tensor>]

Explicitly, $\( "id" ⊗ I_E \) j_w$ maps $U \( 1 \)$ into $\( W ⊗ W \) \( 1 \)$, and twisting by $bb(Q) \( - 1 \)$ gives Equation (7.2). This is the specified untwisted tensor. Its recovery will use the full rational space of Hodge maps to $W ⊗ W$.

=== 7.3 A rational spanning lemma for the two half-spin factors <a-rational-spanning-lemma-for-the-two-half-spin-factors>

The Clifford tensor and the even-module half-spin decomposition are classical; see [37] Proposition 6.3 and Example 6.6. The spanning argument below also uses rational Hodge-Hom descent and both tensor orientations. The following statement is about effective weight-one Hodge structures: their only types are $\( 1 \, 0 \)$ and $\( 0 \, 1 \)$.

#thm([Lemma 7.2.])[
  #emph[Let $\( U \, q \)$ be an eight-dimensional polarized rational Hodge structure of K3 type, of signature $\( 2 \, 6 \)$, and suppose $"Hg" \( U \) = "SO" \( U \, q \)$. Let $W_1 \, W_2$ be polarized effective weight-one Hodge structures and let $W = C^(+) \( U \, q \)$ have its Kuga–Satake Hodge structure. For every nonzero Hodge map 
$ f : U arrow.r W_1 ⊗ W_2 \, $

 put 
$ cal(H) = "Hom"_(upright("HS")) \( W_1 \, W \) ⊗_(bb(Q)) "Hom"_(upright("HS")) \( W_2 \, W \) . $

 If $tau : W ⊗ W arrow.r W ⊗ W$ is the ordinary tensor flip, the rational linear map 
$ Phi : cal(H) xor cal(H) arrow.r "Hom"_(upright("HS")) \( U \, W ⊗ W \) \, $

 defined by 
$ {Phi \( a ⊗ b \, 0 \) = \( a ⊗ b \) compose f \,\
Phi \( 0 \, a ⊗ b \) = tau compose \( a ⊗ b \) compose f $

 is surjective.]
  #emph[If $U subset H^2 \( M \, bb(Q) \)$ for a smooth projective surface $M$, $W_i = H^1 \( C_i \, bb(Q) \)$, $W = H^1 \( A \, bb(Q) \)$, and $f$ is the restriction of a codimension-two algebraic correspondence from $M$ to $C_1 times C_2$, then every map in the target of $Phi$ is induced by a cycle in $"CH"^2 \( M times A times A \)_(bb(Q))$.]
] <final:spin>

#proof[
  We prove surjectivity of the rational map $Phi$ by spanning both complex half-spin tensor orientations. We first isolate the rational orthogonal Lie algebra in the common Hodge group. Weight-one effectivity will restrict the nontrivial constituents of $W_1 \, W_2$ to half-spins and identify their rational intertwiners with Hodge maps to $W$. This allows the tensor-product calculation to be made over $bb(C)$ and its surjectivity to be descended to $bb(Q)$.
  #emph[The rational Hodge group.] Let
  $ G = "Hg" \( W_1 xor W_2 xor W \) . $
  The Hodge group is the connected rational algebraic group generated by the Hodge circle. Any rational invariant subspace of the polarized pure weight-one structure $W_1 xor W_2 xor W$ has a Hodge complement, obtained from its polarization. The complement is again $G$-invariant. Its faithful representation is therefore completely reducible, so $G$ is reductive.
  The embedding in Equation (7.1) realizes $U \( 1 \)$ in $"End" \( W \)$; Tate twists do not change the Hodge-circle action. This gives a representation of $G$ on $U$ preserving $q$. Since $G$ is connected, its image lies in $"SO" \( U \, q \)$. It contains the Hodge circle of $U$ and therefore $"Hg" \( U \) = "SO" \( U \, q \)$, so the map is surjective. Write $frak(g) = "Lie" \( G \)$. The derived Lie algebra of $frak(g)$ is a direct sum of rational simple ideals. Since $frak(s o) \( U \, q \)$ is absolutely simple, exactly one of those ideals maps nontrivially to it: two such images would be commuting copies of the same nonabelian simple algebra. The nonzero map is an isomorphism, and the remaining ideals and the center form its kernel. We obtain rational Lie ideals
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.3)")
   $ frak(g) = frak(s) xor frak(k) \, #h(2em) frak(s) arrow.r^tilde.op frak(s o) \( U \, q \) \, #h(2em) frak(k) = ker #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] frak(g) arrow.r frak(s o) \( U \, q \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <final:lie-splitting>]
  The ideal $frak(s)$ integrates to a connected rational normal semisimple subgroup. Finite central kernels are harmless here; we do not identify a spin representation with a representation of $"SO" \( U \, q \)$.
  #emph[The allowed weight-one constituents.] Normalize the complexified infinitesimal Hodge-circle generator $D$ so that its eigenvalues on weight one are $1 \, - 1$. Its image on $U$ has eigenvalues $2 \, 0 \, dots.h \, 0 \, - 2$. Write $D = D_(frak(s)) + D_(frak(k))$ according to Equation (7.3). Each $frak(s)_(bb(C))$-isotypic summand of $W_(i \, bb(C))$ has the form $R_lambda ⊗ M$, with $R_lambda$ irreducible. The commuting operator $D_(frak(k))$ acts only on $M$. The two projections of the semisimple element $D$ are semisimple, so their eigenvalues add on simultaneous eigenspaces.
  Identify the root system of $frak(s)_(bb(C))$ with $D_4$, with roots $plus.minus e_i plus.minus e_j$, using a Cartan subalgebra in which $D_(frak(s))$ pairs with weights as $2 e_1$. A dominant highest weight has
  $ lambda_1 gt.eq lambda_2 gt.eq lambda_3 gt.eq \| lambda_4 \| \, $
  with coordinates either all integral or all half-integral. Its Weyl orbit contains weights giving the eigenvalues $2 lambda_1$ and $- 2 lambda_1$ for $D_(frak(s))$. Translation by any eigenvalue of the commuting factor cannot reduce this spread. Effectivity of weight one therefore gives $4 lambda_1 lt.eq 2$. The only possibilities are the trivial representation and the two half-spin representations $S_(+) \, S_(-)$, with highest weights $1 / 2 \( e_1 + e_2 + e_3 + e_4 \)$ and $1 / 2 \( e_1 + e_2 + e_3 - e_4 \)$. Their weights are all sign choices $1 / 2 \( plus.minus e_1 plus.minus e_2 plus.minus e_3 plus.minus e_4 \)$ of the respective parities. Each half-spin therefore has both $D_(frak(s))$-eigenvalues $1 \, - 1$. If $mu$ is an eigenvalue of $D_(frak(k))$ on its multiplicity space, both $mu + 1$ and $mu - 1$ must belong to ${ 1 \, - 1 }$; hence $mu = 0$. Semisimplicity now shows that $D_(frak(k))$ is zero on that multiplicity space.
  The rational decompositions
  $ W_i = W_i^(frak(s)) xor W_i^(upright("nt")) \, #h(2em) W_i^(upright("nt")) = frak(s) W_i $
  are $G$-stable. On $W_i^(upright("nt"))$ the entire Hodge-circle generator consequently acts through $frak(s)$. The same assertion holds on $W$. Its Hodge group lies in the rational spin group acting by left multiplication on $C^(+) \( U \, q \)$ and maps onto $"SO" \( U \, q \)$. The spin covering has finite kernel, so the Lie algebra of this image on $W$ maps isomorphically to $frak(s o) \( U \, q \)$; in particular $frak(k)$ acts trivially on $W$.
  For completeness, write $U_(bb(C)) = F xor F^or$ with $F$ maximal isotropic of dimension four. Exterior multiplication and contraction give the Clifford action on $and.big^bullet F$. These operators generate its matrix units, and the even algebra preserves parity. Thus, with $S_(+) = and.big^(upright("even")) F$ and $S_(-) = and.big^(upright("odd")) F$, one has
  $ C^(+) \( U \)_(bb(C)) tilde.eq "End" \( S_(+) \) xor "End" \( S_(-) \) . $
  Since $dim S_(+) = dim S_(-) = 8$, left multiplication gives eight copies of each half-spin. In particular $W$ has no $frak(s)$-invariant summand.
  #emph[Rational Hodge maps and complex multiplicities.] A rational $frak(s)$-intertwiner from $W_i^(upright("nt"))$ to $W$ commutes with $D$ and hence with the connected Hodge circle. Extended by zero on $W_i^(frak(s))$, it is therefore a Hodge map. Conversely every rational Hodge map commutes with $G$ and hence with $frak(s)$. We have proved
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.4)")
   $ "Hom"_(upright("HS")) \( W_i \, W \) & = "Hom"_(frak(s)) \( W_i^(upright("nt")) \, W \) \,\
"Hom"_(upright("HS")) \( U \, W ⊗ W \) & = "Hom"_(frak(s)) \( U \, W ⊗ W \) . $ <final:hom-equality>]
  In the second equality, both Hodge structures have weight two and their Hodge-circle actions factor through $frak(s)$, so the same commutation argument applies. For any two rational $frak(s)$-modules $V_1 \, V_2$ the intertwiner space is the kernel of the rational linear map
  $ "Hom"_(bb(Q)) \( V_1 \, V_2 \) arrow.r "Hom"_(bb(Q)) \( frak(s) ⊗ V_1 \, V_2 \) \, #h(2em) a mapsto #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( x \, v \) mapsto a \( x v \) - x thin a \( v \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
