=== 7.4 Transfer by a rational Hodge isometry <transfer-by-a-rational-hodge-isometry>

#thm([Proposition 7.3.])[
  #emph[Let $b$ be a construction point of Lemma 29, and let $S$ be a projective K3 surface equipped with a rational Hodge isometry $r_T : T_S arrow.r^tilde.op U_b = T_(S_b^0)$ for the cup-product forms. For every full even-Clifford model $A$ of $T_S$, every rational nonisotropic right factor $w$, and every rational polarization $E$, there is a cycle 
$ Gamma_S in "CH"^2 \( S times A times A \)_(bb(Q)) \, #h(2em) \( Gamma_S \)_(*) x = iota_(w \, E) \( x \) quad \( x in T_S \) \, $

 where the equality holds in $H^2 \( A times A \, bb(Q) \)$.]
] <final:generic>

#proof[
  The assumed $r_T$ uses the form on $U_b$ inherited from $Y$, which the auxiliary K3 realizes isometrically. The factor-two pullback to $X_b$ enters only the curve correspondence of Lemma 29 and is not part of this isometry.
  Choose markings of the full second cohomology spaces by $Lambda_(upright(K 3) \, bb(Q))$. They turn $r_T$ into an isometry between two nondegenerate rational subspaces of this one quadratic space. Witt’s Extension Theorem [30] Corollary 7.2.8 extends it to an isometry of $Lambda_(upright(K 3) \, bb(Q))$. Returning through the markings gives
  $ r : H^2 \( S \, bb(Q) \) arrow.r^tilde.op H^2 \( S_b^0 \, bb(Q) \) . $
  The isometry $r$ maps the orthogonal complement of $T_S$ to the orthogonal complement of $T_(S_b^0)$. These are exactly $"NS" \( S \)_(bb(Q))$ and $"NS" \( S_b^0 \)_(bb(Q))$, both of type $\( 1 \, 1 \)$. Since its restriction $r_T$ is Hodge, $r$ is therefore a rational Hodge isometry on the full second cohomology. Buskin’s Theorem [9] Theorem 1.1, applied to these smooth projective complex K3 surfaces, gives a cycle
  $ R in "CH"^2 \( S times S_b^0 \)_(bb(Q)) \, #h(2em) R_(*) \|_(H^2 \( S \, bb(Q) \)) = r . $
  The hypotheses here are precisely full rational Hodge isometry and projectivity; the integral Néron–Severi lattice of $S_b^0$ is not required to equal $P$.
  Compose $R$ with the cycle $Z$ of Lemma 29. The resulting codimension-two correspondence induces the injection
  $ f_S : T_S arrow.r^(r_T) U_b arrow.r^f H^1 \( C_1 \, bb(Q) \) ⊗ H^1 \( C_2 \, bb(Q) \) . $
  At these periods $"Hg" \( T_S \) = "SO" \( T_S \)$. Apply Lemma 30 to $f_S$ and to the given full even-Clifford model $W = H^1 \( A \, bb(Q) \)$ of dimension $128$. The prescribed map $iota_(w \, E)$ of Equation (7.2) belongs to the target of $Phi$. A rational preimage under $Phi$, realized by the divisor products and signed interchange in that lemma, supplies $Gamma_S$ with exactly the asserted action. In particular the equality is on all of $T_S$ and has no extra component in the other Künneth summands of $H^2 \( A times A \, bb(Q) \)$.
